            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a limb, then the Latin for joints."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: vēna, vēnae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vēna, vēnae — \"a vein\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Sanguis per vēnās currit. Blood runs through the veins."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, first declension. Also a vein of metal, or of talent. English vein."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "vēna is a native Latin noun; Lewis & Short guesses a link with vehō \"to carry\", but the origin is not settled. English vein comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
